I inspected the full Experiment_1.ipynb, including the synthetic-data generation, PGLA teacher/student training, latent-dimension sweep, vision conditioning, ablations, baselines, and benchmark outputs. The notebook is a Physics-Grounded Latent Action (PGLA) Milestone 1 experiment implemented in PyTorch and run with a Tesla T4 GPU. 


Below is a README you can paste directly into README.md.

Antencoder-AntVLA
Physics-Grounded Latent Action (PGLA)
This repository contains the first experimental milestone of Antencoder-AntVLA, exploring a Physics-Grounded Latent Action (PGLA) representation for vision-language-action (VLA) systems.
The central idea is to learn a compact latent representation of robot actions from physical state + action, and then teach a language encoder to reproduce that representation from natural-language commands.

In simplified form:

                 ┌────────────────────┐
                 │   Vision / State   │
                 └─────────┬──────────┘
                           │
                           │
                 ┌─────────▼──────────┐
                 │  Physical Teacher  │
                 │      V + A         │
                 └─────────┬──────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
                      Latent z
                           ▲
                           │
                 ┌─────────┴──────────┐
                 │ Language Student   │
                 │       Text         │
                 └─────────▲──────────┘
                           │
                    "push the red cube right"

The experiment investigates whether language can be mapped into a physically meaningful latent action space and whether that representation can subsequently be combined with visual information for action prediction.
The complete experiment is implemented in Experiment_1.ipynb.

Overview
The experiment consists of several stages:
Construct a synthetic tabletop manipulation environment.
Generate natural-language commands.
Convert commands into 7-dimensional physical actions.
Train a Physical Teacher to encode vision/state + action into a latent representation.
Train a Language Student to reproduce the teacher's latent representation from text.
Evaluate latent alignment and action prediction.
Sweep the latent dimensionality.
Add a vision-conditioned action model.
Perform a vision-shuffling ablation.
Compare against language-only, vision-only, and conventional language+vision baselines.
Benchmark training throughput and parameter counts.
Experiment 1: Synthetic Physical World
The first milestone uses a procedurally generated synthetic environment rather than a real robotics dataset.
Each scene contains three colored cubes:

Red
Blue
Green
Each cube is represented by a 3D position:
(x, y, z)

Positions are randomly sampled from the workspace.
The experiment therefore creates paired:

scene + language command → physical action

samples automatically. 

Supported commands
The synthetic language contains four high-level actions:
Action	Example
pick	pick up the red cube
push	push the blue cube right
pull	pull the green cube backward
place	place the red cube

For push and pull, the available directions are:
left
right
forward
backward

The vocabulary contains 18 tokens including special tokens, actions, colors, the object name, directions, and simple grammatical words. 

Action Representation
Each physical action is represented using a 7-dimensional vector:
[x, y, z, roll, pitch, yaw, gripper]

where:
x, y, z represent the target position.
roll, pitch, yaw represent orientation.
gripper represents the gripper state.
For example, a pick action uses the target cube position and activates the gripper, while push and pull modify the cube position according to the requested direction.
The synthetic action generator is implemented directly in the notebook. 


Synthetic Vision Representation
Rather than rendering RGB images, the experiment converts the scene state into a compact synthetic vision feature.
The three cube positions are concatenated:

red position
+ blue position
+ green position

giving a 9-dimensional scene representation.
This is projected into a:

512-dimensional vision feature

with small Gaussian noise added to simulate imperfect visual observations. 

This allows the experiment to study language/vision/action relationships without requiring a full robotics simulator.

PGLA Architecture
1. Physical Teacher
The Physical Teacher learns a latent representation directly from physical information:
Vision + Action → Latent Action

The teacher contains:
Vision encoder
Action encoder
Feature fusion network
Latent representation
Action prediction head
The default latent dimension is:
128

The teacher is trained to reconstruct the physical action, encouraging its latent representation to retain information necessary for action execution.
2. Language Student
The Language Student receives only the language command:
Text → Latent Action

It consists of:
Token Embedding
      ↓
Bidirectional GRU
      ↓
Mean Pooling
      ↓
MLP Projection
      ↓
Latent z

The default architecture uses:
Embedding dimension: 96
GRU hidden dimension: 192
Bidirectional GRU
Projection dimension: 256
Latent dimension: 128
The final latent representation uses a Tanh activation. 

Knowledge Distillation
The student is trained against the latent representation produced by the frozen Physical Teacher.
The training objective combines two terms:

L = L_latent + 0.5 × L_action

where:
L_latent encourages the language representation to match the teacher representation.
L_action encourages the student representation to remain useful for physical action prediction.
This creates the following training process:
                 Physical Teacher
                Vision + Action
                      │
                      ▼
                 Teacher latent
                      │
                      │ latent loss
                      ▼
Language ───────► Student latent
                      │
                      ▼
                 Action Head
                      │
                      ▼
                Predicted Action

The teacher is frozen while training the student. 

Dataset
The dataset is generated entirely in memory.
Split	Samples
Training	30,000
Validation	5,000
Test	5,000
Total	40,000

The notebook uses a batch size of:
256

and fixes the random seed to 42 for reproducibility. 

No external dataset download is required for this experiment.

Training Configuration
Default configuration:
Parameter	Value
Vision dimension	512
Action dimension	7
Latent dimension	128
Embedding dimension	96
Hidden dimension	192
Maximum sequence length	12
Batch size	256
Training samples	30,000
Validation samples	5,000
Test samples	5,000
Teacher epochs	20
Student epochs	20
Teacher learning rate	3e-4
Student learning rate	3e-4
Optimizer	AdamW
Weight decay	1e-4

The notebook automatically uses CUDA when available. The recorded experiment was run on a Tesla T4 using PyTorch 2.11.0+cu128. 

Latent-Dimension Sweep
One of the main experiments evaluates different latent-space sizes:
32
64
128
256
512

The experiment measures:
Teacher position MAE
Student position MAE
Student rotation MAE
Gripper accuracy
Latent MSE
Latent cosine similarity
Action-probe accuracy
Results from the notebook:
Latent Dim	Student Params	Student Position MAE	Rotation MAE	Gripper Acc.	Latent MSE	Cosine Similarity	Action Probe
32	541,991	0.2911	0.00122	1.000	0.02819	0.9751	1.000
64	554,311	0.2898	0.00435	1.000	0.03051	0.9715	1.000
128	578,951	0.2899	0.00968	1.000	0.03514	0.9648	0.976
256	628,231	0.2877	0.01169	1.000	0.04029	0.9557	1.000
512	726,791	0.2917	0.01995	1.000	0.04383	0.9487	1.000

The results suggest that increasing the latent dimensionality does not automatically improve language-to-action performance in this synthetic setting. In particular, the smaller latent spaces achieve very strong teacher-student cosine alignment while using substantially fewer parameters. 

Latent Representation Analysis
The notebook also analyzes the geometry of the learned latent space.
Latents are collected for the test set and grouped by action type:

pick
push
pull
place

The 5,000 test examples produce:
[5000, 128]

latent representations.
The experiment computes action-specific latent centroids to investigate whether different physical actions occupy distinguishable regions of the representation space. 


An additional linear probe is trained on frozen latent representations to determine how easily action type can be decoded from the latent space.

This provides a simple test of whether action semantics are explicitly represented in the learned embedding.

Vision-Conditioned PGLA
After training the language student, the experiment extends the architecture to incorporate vision.
The resulting model follows:

Language → z_language ─┐
                       ├→ Fusion → Action
Vision   → z_vision ───┘

The language encoder is reused and frozen.
The vision branch maps the 512-dimensional visual feature into the latent space:

512 → 256 → latent_dim

The language and vision representations are concatenated and passed through a fusion network before the final action head. 

Vision Ablation
To test whether the vision input actually contributes useful information, the notebook performs a controlled ablation.
During evaluation, visual features are randomly shuffled across samples in a batch:

correct language + incorrect vision

The resulting position error is compared with normal evaluation.
Result
Condition	Position MAE
Normal vision	0.2405
Shuffled vision	0.3188

The degradation from 0.2405 to 0.3188 indicates that the model is using information contained in the visual representation rather than relying exclusively on language. 

Baselines
The experiment evaluates several alternatives.
Language-only baseline
A conventional language encoder is trained directly for action prediction:
Language → Latent → Action

The baseline uses a bidirectional GRU with a projection layer and does not use the PGLA teacher representation.
Recorded test results:

Metric	Language Baseline
Position MAE	0.2867
Rotation MAE	0.00190
Gripper Accuracy	1.000



Vision-only baseline
A separate model predicts actions using only the synthetic vision feature:
Vision → Action

Recorded test results:
Metric	Vision-only
Position MAE	0.2715
Rotation MAE	0.00194
Gripper Accuracy	0.7512



Language + Vision baseline
A controlled baseline combines a conventional language encoder and the same vision/fusion/action-head structure used by the PGLA vision-conditioned model.
This provides a more direct comparison because the main architectural components are kept similar while replacing the PGLA language representation with a standard language representation.


Parameter counts:

Model	Parameters
PGLA Language + Vision	876,942
Conventional Language + Vision	1,723,527

The PGLA model therefore uses substantially fewer parameters in this experiment. 

Initial PGLA Student Results
For the default 128-dimensional PGLA representation, the recorded student evaluation gives:
Metric	Result
Latent MSE	0.02454
Latent cosine similarity	0.97685
Action MAE	1.52806
Position MAE	0.29069
Rotation MAE	0.00383
Gripper accuracy	1.000

The high cosine similarity indicates that the language student successfully approximates the teacher's latent representation, although the action prediction error shows that latent alignment alone does not guarantee accurate continuous action reconstruction. 

Training Speed
The notebook also benchmarks training throughput.
For a 5-epoch benchmark:

Model	Time	Time / Epoch	Samples / Second
PGLA Student	6.46 s	1.29 s	23,209
Language Baseline	7.49 s	1.50 s	20,021

The PGLA student achieved higher measured throughput in this benchmark. These measurements are hardware-dependent and should not be interpreted as universal speedups. 

Installation
The experiment requires Python and the following main packages:
pip install torch numpy scikit-learn

A CUDA-enabled PyTorch installation is recommended for reproducing the recorded experiment.
The notebook automatically selects:

device = torch.device(
    "cuda" if torch.cuda.is_available() else "cpu"
)

Running the Experiment
Option 1: Google Colab
The notebook was designed to run in Google Colab with GPU acceleration.
Open:

Experiment_1.ipynb

Then select:

Runtime → Change runtime type → GPU

A Tesla T4 was used for the recorded experiment.
Option 2: Local Jupyter
Clone the repository:
git clone https://github.com/nicopicomoco-sekihan-80/Antencoder-AntVLA.git
cd Antencoder-AntVLA

Install dependencies:
pip install torch numpy scikit-learn jupyter

Launch Jupyter:
jupyter notebook

Open:
Experiment_1.ipynb

and execute the cells from top to bottom.
Saved Models
At the end of the experiment, the notebook saves the learned encoders:
pgla_encoder.pt
language_encoder.pt

The PGLA encoder is saved from the trained student, while the second file contains the conventional language baseline encoder. 

Repository Structure
The repository currently centers on the experimental notebook:
Antencoder-AntVLA/
│
├── Experiment_1.ipynb
├── pgla_encoder.pt          # generated after training
├── language_encoder.pt      # generated after training
└── README.md

The .pt files are generated artifacts and may not exist until the notebook has been executed.
Experimental Takeaways
The first milestone provides several useful observations:
1. Language can approximate a physically trained latent space
The language student reaches a high teacher-student cosine similarity, demonstrating that a compact latent representation learned from physical action information can be approximated from language alone. 

2. Latent dimensionality has a trade-off
Increasing the latent dimension increases model size but does not consistently improve the student metrics. The 32- and 64-dimensional representations achieve particularly strong latent cosine alignment with fewer parameters. 

3. Vision provides additional information
Shuffling the visual features substantially increases position error:
0.2405 → 0.3188

suggesting that the vision-conditioned model uses scene-specific information. 
4. The PGLA representation is compact
The PGLA language+vision model has approximately 877K parameters, compared with approximately 1.72M for the controlled conventional language+vision baseline. 
5. This is a synthetic proof-of-concept
The current experiment should be interpreted as a controlled milestone rather than a demonstration on a real robot. The scenes, language commands, visual features, and physical actions are procedurally generated.
The next step is therefore to test whether the same representation-learning principle transfers to realistic visual observations, robot trajectories, and real or simulated manipulation environments.

Limitations
This experiment has several important limitations:
The visual input is a synthetic 512-dimensional feature rather than an RGB image.
The language vocabulary is very small.
Only four action types are supported.
Actions are single-step 7D vectors rather than temporally extended trajectories.
The physical dynamics are simplified deterministic rules.
No real robot hardware is evaluated.
No standard robotics benchmark is used.
The reported results come from a single synthetic experimental setup.
Consequently, the results demonstrate the feasibility of the proposed representation-learning setup, but they should not be interpreted as evidence of real-world robotic performance.
Future Work
Potential next steps include:
Replace synthetic vision features with real RGB observations.
Integrate a pretrained vision encoder.
Move from single-step actions to action sequences.
Introduce temporal trajectory prediction.
Evaluate on simulated manipulation environments.
Evaluate on real robot demonstrations.
Investigate contrastive and metric-learning objectives for the latent space.
Improve language compositionality.
Test unseen object/color/action combinations.
Study whether the learned latent space transfers across tasks.
Compare against established VLA architectures.
Investigate whether the PGLA representation can serve as a reusable interface between language models and robot controllers.
